::ALG2_U02_L03_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the end behavior of f(x) = x<sup>4</sup> - 3x + 1? {
=both ends rise #Correct. Even degree with positive leading coefficient means both ends rise.
~both ends fall #This would require a negative leading coefficient.
~left falls, right rises #This is odd positive behavior.
~left rises, right falls #This is odd negative behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the end behavior of f(x) = -x<sup>3</sup> + 2x? {
~both ends rise #Odd degree has opposite end behavior.
~left falls, right rises #This is positive odd behavior.
=left rises, right falls #Correct. Odd degree with negative leading coefficient rises left and falls right.
~both ends fall #Even negative behavior falls both ends.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which feature controls end behavior most directly? {
~constant term #The constant term does not control long-run behavior.
=degree and leading coefficient #Correct. The leading term dominates for large x-values.
~y-intercept #The y-intercept is local information.
~middle term #Middle terms do not dominate end behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = 5x<sup>7</sup> - 4, what happens as x increases without bound? {
~f(x) decreases without bound #Positive odd degree rises right.
~f(x) approaches -4 #The constant term does not control end behavior.
~f(x) stays constant #The function is not constant.
=f(x) increases without bound #Correct. Positive odd degree rises to the right.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = -2x<sup>8</sup> + x, what happens on both ends? {
~both ends rise #Positive even degree would rise.
=both ends fall #Correct. Even degree with negative leading coefficient means both ends fall.
~left falls, right rises #This is positive odd behavior.
~left rises, right falls #This is negative odd behavior.
}

